    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "While a pale Senya insists he merely ate expired yogurt in the morning, Mikhail Jekovich leads the stubborn pony into the staff locker room for the night. Nearby, Katya apologizes to Denis for not speaking with Nikita before his trip.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это вообще была не рыба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Утром я просроченный йогурт съел.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Давай заходи сюда, упрямая лошадь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Веду его сюда на ночлег.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нельзя во дворе его оставить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Зрелище тут не очень приятное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ничего, он до утра потерпит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Катя, ты поговорила с Никитой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, сегодня опять не вышло.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я полдня провела у отца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А потом Никита уехал в Питер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не обижайся, для меня это важно.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Это вообще была не рыба?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Was that not the fish at all?' Mikhail anchors the line on 'this', while 'fish' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Утром я просроченный йогурт съел.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the morning I ate expired yogurt.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'in the morning' and finishing on 'ate'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Давай заходи сюда, упрямая лошадь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Come on inside here, stubborn horse.' Notice how Russian expresses 'come on' and 'horse' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Веду его сюда на ночлег.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am leading him in here for the night.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'led' before landing on 'overnight stay'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Нельзя во дворе его оставить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I cannot leave him in the courtyard.' Mikhail brings out the contrast between 'one must not' and 'yard' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Зрелище тут не очень приятное.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The sight in here is not very pleasant.' Mikhail anchors the line on 'sight', while 'something pleasant' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "mikhail",
      "t": "Ничего, он до утра потерпит.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is fine, he will endure until morning.' The grammar packs 'nothing' and 'will endure' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "denis",
      "t": "Катя, ты поговорила с Никитой?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Katya, did you talk with Nikita?' The meaning hinges on 'Katya' working together with 'Nikita' in spoken Russian. Katya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, сегодня опять не вышло.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, it did not work out again today.' Katya brings out the contrast between 'no' and 'tonight' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я полдня провела у отца.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I spent half the day with my father.' In Russian, the idea of 'I' flows naturally into 'father' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А потом Никита уехал в Питер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And then Nikita left for Saint Petersburg.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'and' and finishing on 'Saint Petersburg'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не обижайся, для меня это важно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not be offended, this is important to me.' The negative particle sits right before the verb meaning 'be offended' to negate the statement. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
